`,s.jsxs(z,{title:"Reduce per image, then average — not over the whole batch",children:[s.jsx(e.p,{children:"Summing the intersection across the entire batch lets a large object in one image compensate for a missed small object in another: the batch-level Dice barely moves, so the network is never punished for the miss."}),s.jsxs(e.p,{children:["Computing Dice ",s.jsx(e.strong,{children:"per image"})," and averaging makes every image count equally, which is what you want when object size varies. The ",s.jsx(e.code,{children:"dims=(1,2,3)"})," above does exactly that. For multi-class, reduce per class as well and average — that is macro-Dice, and it is the number that exposes a class the model is quietly ignoring."]})]}),`
`,s.jsx(v,{items:[{q:"Why does soft Dice need an epsilon in both numerator and denominator?",a:"To handle the empty-target case. If the ground truth has no foreground and the model correctly predicts none, the ratio is 0/0 — a nan that propagates through the batch. With epsilon it becomes 1/1, a perfect score for a correct empty prediction. It also damps the wild gradient swings that a tiny denominator causes on very small objects."},{q:"Why compute Dice per image and average, rather than over the whole batch at once?",a:"Because a batch-level sum lets a large well-segmented object in one image mask a completely missed small object in another — the pooled intersection barely changes, so the network is never punished. Per-image reduction then averaging weights every image equally, which is what you want when object sizes vary. The same argument extends to per-class reduction for multi-class macro-Dice."}]}),`
